      "id": 1,
      "text": "Prophetstown was named for Wabokieshiek, the Winnebago prophet who once lived on the land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prophetstown%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits on the site of a Winnebago village that the Illinois volunteers destroyed on May 10, 1832, sparking the Black Hawk War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prophetstown%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An 1825 treaty marked a Winnebago village about 40 miles above the Rock River as a boundary point, placing Prophetstown at a historic frontier line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
